{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_553ewgbg2dk6vgqvnu24rwa5qqwrrpdcfohzuiml5qtutltezfba","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01SgxpT63DytvvnYD68YkSKg","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-fDPIPe/translation/examples/probe.rs","old_string":"//! Exit code 0 on completion; the caller compares stdout bytes *and* exit\n//! status between the C and the Rust run.","new_string":"//! Exit code 0 on completion; the caller compares stdout bytes *and* exit\n//! status between the C and the Rust run.\n//!\n//! An optional second argument `flush` makes the probe `fflush(NULL)` after\n//! *every* op. That matters for the out-of-bounds `bad()` indices: `stdout` is\n//! block-buffered when it is a pipe, so a call that prints ten lines and *then*\n//! dies on `ret` loses all ten to the un-flushed buffer. Flushing per op\n//! separates \"what did the library print\" from \"did the process survive\"."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3106,"cache_read_input_tokens":74752,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3106,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"508d9285-f2f8-4563-9ede-ba90eaf98e51","uuid":"45d3e996-ab1f-4778-aec6-0919a9965801","timestamp":"2026-08-28T11:34:13.209Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01SgxpT63DytvvnYD68YkSKg","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-fDPIPe/translation/examples/probe.rs has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"508d9285-f2f8-4563-9ede-ba90eaf98e51","uuid":"82028b1e-3876-41a0-b36e-3f62cbfe52a5","timestamp":"2026-08-28T11:34:13.223Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-fDPIPe/translation/examples/probe.rs","oldString":"//! Exit code 0 on completion; the caller compares stdout bytes *and* exit\n//! status between the C and the Rust run.","newString":"//! Exit code 0 on completion; the caller compares stdout bytes *and* exit\n//! status between the C and the Rust run.\n//!\n//! An optional second argument `flush` makes the probe `fflush(NULL)` after\n//! *every* op. That matters for the out-of-bounds `bad()` indices: `stdout` is\n//! block-buffered when it is a pipe, so a call that prints ten lines and *then*\n//! dies on `ret` loses all ten to the un-flushed buffer. Flushing per op\n//! separates \"what did the library print\" from \"did the process survive\".","originalFile":"//! Out-of-process differential probe.\n//!\n//! Usage: `probe <path-to-shared-object>` with a newline-separated op script on\n//! stdin.  The probe `dlopen`s exactly ONE shared object (so the identically\n//! named `driver` symbols of the C and the Rust build can never interpose on\n//! each other) and replays the script through it.  Everything the library\n//! prints goes to the probe's stdout verbatim; the probe itself only ever\n//! writes to stderr.\n//!\n//! Script grammar (one op per line, blank lines ignored):\n//!\n//! ```text\n//!   L <hex>          printLine(cstr(hex bytes))\n//!   N                printLine(NULL)\n//!   I <int>          printIntLine(int)\n//!   B <int>          bad(int)\n//!   G <int>          good(int)\n//!   D <int> <int>    driver(goodData, badData)\n//! ```\n//!\n//! Exit code 0 on completion; the caller compares stdout bytes *and* exit\n//! status between the C and the Rust run.\n\nuse std::ffi::{c_char, c_int, c_void};\nuse std::io::Read;\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    /// `fflush(NULL)` flushes every open output stream, including the `stdout`\n    /// the freshly `dlopen`ed library's `printf` calls wrote into.\n    fn fflush(stream: *mut c_void) -> c_int;\n}\n\ntype FnPtr = unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*const c_char);\ntype FnInt = unsafe extern \"C\" fn(c_int);\ntype FnIntInt = unsafe extern \"C\" fn(c_int, c_int);\n\nfn unhex(s: &str) -> Vec<u8> {\n    let b = s.as_bytes();\n    assert!(b.len() % 2 == 0, \"odd hex length\");\n    (0..b.len() / 2)\n        .map(|i| u8::from_str_radix(&s[2 * i..2 * i + 2], 16).expect(\"bad hex\"))\n        .collect()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut args = std::env::args_os().skip(1);\n    let lib_path = args.next().expect(\"usage: probe <lib.so>\");\n\n    let mut script = String::new();\n    std::io::stdin()\n        .read_to_string(&mut script)\n        .expect(\"read script\");\n\n    unsafe {\n        let lib = libloading::Library::new(&lib_path)\n            .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"dlopen {lib_path:?}: {e}\"));\n\n        let print_line: libloading::Symbol<FnPtr> = lib.get(b\"printLine\\0\").expect(\"printLine\");\n        let print_int_line: libloading::Symbol<FnInt> =\n            lib.get(b\"printIntLine\\0\").expect(\"printIntLine\");\n        let bad: libloading::Symbol<FnInt> = lib.get(b\"bad\\0\").expect(\"bad\");\n        let good: libloading::Symbol<FnInt> = lib.get(b\"good\\0\").expect(\"good\");\n        let driver: libloading::Symbol<FnIntInt> = lib.get(b\"driver\\0\").expect(\"driver\");\n\n        for line in script.lines() {\n            let line = line.trim();\n            if line.is_empty() {\n                continue;\n            }\n            let mut it = line.split_whitespace();\n            let op = it.next().unwrap();\n            match op {\n                \"L\" => {\n                    let mut bytes = unhex(it.next().unwrap_or(\"\"));\n                    bytes.push(0); // NUL terminate\n                    print_line(bytes.as_ptr() as *const c_char);\n                }\n                \"N\" => print_line(std::ptr::null()),\n                \"I\" => print_int_line(it.next().unwrap().parse::<i32>().unwrap() as c_int),\n                \"B\" => bad(it.next().unwrap().parse::<i32>().unwrap() as c_int),\n                \"G\" => good(it.next().unwrap().parse::<i32>().unwrap() as c_int),\n                \"D\" => {\n                    let a = it.next().unwrap().parse::<i32>().unwrap() as c_int;\n                    let b = it.next().unwrap().parse::<i32>().unwrap() as c_int;\n                    driver(a, b);\n                }\n                other => panic!(\"unknown op {other:?}\"),\n            }\n        }\n\n        fflush(std::ptr::null_mut());\n    }\n}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":20,"oldLines":6,"newStart":20,"newLines":12,"lines":[" //!"," //! Exit code 0 on completion; the caller compares stdout bytes *and* exit"," //! status between the C and the Rust run.","+//!","+//! An optional second argument `flush` makes the probe `fflush(NULL)` after","+//! *every* op. That matters for the out-of-bounds `bad()` indices: `stdout` is","+//! block-buffered when it is a pipe, so a call that prints ten lines and *then*","+//! dies on `ret` loses all ten to the un-flushed buffer. Flushing per op","+//! separates \"what did the library print\" from \"did the process survive\"."," "," use std::ffi::{c_char, c_int, c_void};"," use std::io::Read;"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
